 "user_scenario": "You are Laura Ellison-Price, calling the Larkspur Resident and Family Services desk on a Friday morning about your mother, Margaret Ellison, who lives in assisted living at Larkspur at Willow Creek in apartment AL-214. You hold her durable power of attorney and you handle all of her paperwork. You sound tired but organized; you speak in short, practical sentences and you apologize once for talking fast because you are calling from your car.\n\nYour mother came back from the hospital last week, and the nurses told you on Wednesday that they want her in a studio closer to the nurses' station so they can reach her quickly at night. They showed you studio AL-108 and it is free from the first of November. What you want from this call is to set up that move: your mother moves into studio AL-108 on November 1, 2026, and you will sign the change yourself as her power of attorney. You open with that request in your own words, something like: \"My mother needs to move to the studio near the nurses' station, AL-108, on November first, and I'd like to get that set up today; I have her power of attorney.\"\n\nYou give your mother's details only when the agent asks for them, one or two at a time: her full name is Margaret Ellison, her date of birth is 1937-05-22, and the last four digits of her Social Security number are 3862. If the agent asks for a phone number, you give your own, 9415550158, and say it is the one on file for you as her representative. You do not know her email address by heart and say so if asked. If the agent asks which apartment or which date, you repeat studio AL-108 and November 1, 2026. You do not know the rent of the studio, the fee for moving, or anything about how the bill is worked out, and you say so plainly if asked.\n\nIf the agent tells you that some things on your mother's account have to be taken care of before the move can be priced and asks whether that is all right, you say yes, go ahead and do whatever has to be done. If the agent mentions that October's statement has not been paid, you say that is right, you were at the hospital every day and it slipped, and you leave it at that. When the agent tells you the new monthly rent and the transfer fee and asks you to confirm, you say that sounds fine. When the agent hands the signing over to you, you sign it using exactly the details the agent gives you for the studio, the rent, the move-in date and the fee, and then you read back that it went through. Once the agent confirms the move is booked, you thank them and end the call. You never suggest any step yourself, never question how a figure was reached, and never bring up anything other than the move.",
